We'll build this step by step using basic React Native logic: useState, useEffect, TextInput, .find(), .filter(), .map(), and simple functions. No advanced hooks or complicated abstractions.

I've also looked at your uploaded BottomSheetModal.jsx. You already have a reusable bottom sheet that accepts visible, onClose, title, and children. We'll keep that component and build the location selector inside it. 
BottomSheetModal.jsx

Step 1 — Understand the behavior before coding
Your location data has three levels:

State

Karnataka

City

Bangalore · Mysore · Mangalore

Location

Indiranagar · Koramangala · Whitefield

The screen should behave like this:

Show the list of states.

Tap a state to show only its cities.

Tap a city to show only its locations.

Tap a location to select it.

Use the breadcrumbs to go back to any level.

Search by state, city, or location name.

If the user closes the modal without completing a new selection, preserve the previously selected location.

Step 2 — Separate browsing from the actual selection
This is the most important part of the logic.

You need two kinds of state: the user's current browsing level and the confirmed location.

State variable

What it means

states

Data received from the API or JSON file

selectedState

State currently being browsed

selectedCity

City currently being browsed

selectedLocation

The final, confirmed location

searchText

What the user types into the search bar

Why separate them?

Imagine the user has already selected:

Karnataka → Bangalore → Koramangala

They open the modal and browse Karnataka → Mysore, but then close it without choosing a location.

If you immediately overwrite the confirmed selection when they tap Mysore, your previous location is lost. Instead, browsing changes only the browsing state; the confirmed selection changes only when a location is tapped.

Step 3 — Create the basic state variables
Create LocationSelector.jsx. First, import the React Native components you need:

import React, { useState } from 'react';
import {
  View,
  Text,
  TextInput,
  FlatList,
  TouchableOpacity,
  StyleSheet,
} from 'react-native';

Now create the component and its state variables:

const LocationSelector = ({ states, onSelectLocation }) => {
  const [selectedState, setSelectedState] = useState(null);
  const [selectedCity, setSelectedCity] = useState(null);
  const [searchText, setSearchText] = useState('');

  return (
    <View style={{ flex: 1 }}>
      {/* We will build the UI here */}
    </View>
  );
};

export default LocationSelector;

Let's understand these variables.

selectedState starts as null because the user hasn't opened a state yet.

selectedCity starts as null because the user hasn't opened a city yet.

searchText stores whatever the user types.

Important: These variables control navigation inside the modal. They do not change the location already saved by the parent screen.

Step 4 — Decide which list to display
Think through the problem first:

If no state is selected, display states.

If a state is selected but no city is selected, display cities.

If both state and city are selected, display locations.

We can express this with three simple conditions.

const cityList =
  states.find((item) => item.state_id === selectedState?.state_id)
    ?.cities ?? [];

const locationList =
  cityList.find((item) => item.city_id === selectedCity?.city_id)
    ?.locations ?? [];

These lines find the children of the selected state and city.

Now decide which list should be displayed:

let displayList = [];

if (!selectedState) {
  displayList = states;
} else if (!selectedCity) {
  displayList = cityList;
} else {
  displayList = locationList;
}

Notice that we don't need a separate level state variable. We can determine the current level from the selections we already have.

Step 5 — Render the list
Use FlatList to display whichever list displayList contains.

<FlatList
  data={displayList}
  keyExtractor={(item) =>
    String(
      item.state_id ??
      item.city_id ??
      item.location_id
    )
  }
  renderItem={({ item }) => {
    const name =
      item.name ??
      item.city_name ??
      item.location_name;

    return (
      <TouchableOpacity
        onPress={() => {
          if (!selectedState) {
            setSelectedState(item);
          } else if (!selectedCity) {
            setSelectedCity(item);
          } else {
            onSelectLocation(item);
          }
        }}
        style={styles.locationCard}
      >
        <Text>{name}</Text>
      </TouchableOpacity>
    );
  }}
/>

Why does this work?
The onPress function checks what the user is currently browsing.

First tap: selectedState is empty, so the tapped item becomes the selected state.

Second tap: a state exists but no city exists, so the tapped item becomes the selected city.

Third tap: both exist, so the tapped item is the final location.

One thing to remember: onSelectLocation(item) is called only at the last level. We will connect it to your parent screen in a later step.

Step 6 — Add the clickable breadcrumbs
The breadcrumb tells users where they are and allows them to navigate backwards.

For example:

All states

Karnataka

Bangalore

Tap All states to go back to states, or Karnataka to go back to its cities.

Render it with TouchableOpacity:

<View style={styles.breadcrumbs}>
  <TouchableOpacity
    onPress={() => {
      setSelectedState(null);
      setSelectedCity(null);
    }}
  >
    <Text>All states</Text>
  </TouchableOpacity>

  {selectedState && (
    <>
      <Text> › </Text>

      <TouchableOpacity
        onPress={() => {
          setSelectedCity(null);
        }}
      >
        <Text>{selectedState.name}</Text>
      </TouchableOpacity>
    </>
  )}

  {selectedCity && (
    <>
      <Text> › </Text>
      <Text>{selectedCity.city_name}</Text>
    </>
  )}
</View>

The important logic is:

All states: clear both browsing selections.

State: clear only the city selection.

City: display it as the current breadcrumb.

Because the displayed list depends on these values, React automatically renders the appropriate list after you tap a breadcrumb.

Step 7 — Add the search bar
This is where we need to think a little more carefully.

If the user types Karnataka, the result should be the state. If they type Bangalore, the result should identify the city. If they type Koramangala, the result should identify the location.

A simple .filter() over the currently displayed list is not enough because the search needs to look through all three levels.

Start with the input:

<TextInput
  placeholder="Search state, city or location"
  placeholderTextColor="#777777"
  value={searchText}
  onChangeText={setSearchText}
  style={styles.searchBar}
/>

Then, before writing the filter, ask yourself:

If searchText is Koramangala, how can we find that location and also retrieve its parent city and state?

The approach is to loop through the states, then their cities, then their locations. When a name matches, keep the matching result together with its parent information. This also allows a search result to open the correct state or city when tapped.

We'll implement that after the basic list and breadcrumbs work.

Step 8 — Prevent incomplete selections from replacing the old location
This is the rule that protects your existing selection.

Keep the confirmed location in the parent screen:

const [confirmedLocation, setConfirmedLocation] = useState(null);

When the user taps a final location:

const handleLocationSelect = (location) => {
  setConfirmedLocation(location);
  setModalVisible(false);
};

Pass the handler to the selector:

<LocationSelector
  states={states}
  onSelectLocation={handleLocationSelect}
/>

Do not update confirmedLocation when the user taps a state or city. Do not clear it when the user navigates backwards or closes the modal.

This means that if the user opens the modal, explores another state, and closes the modal without choosing a final location, the previously confirmed selection stays intact.

You should also make sure the final selection contains a valid location_id before confirming it.

