Step 2: Build a list of matching locations
The easiest beginner-friendly approach is to loop through the states, cities, and locations using forEach().

const getSearchResults = () => {
  let results = [];

  states.forEach((state) => {
    state.cities.forEach((city) => {
      city.locations.forEach((location) => {
        const search = searchText.toLowerCase().trim();

        if (
          state.name.toLowerCase().includes(search) ||
          city.city_name.toLowerCase().includes(search) ||
          location.location_name.toLowerCase().includes(search)
        ) {
          results.push({
            ...location,
            state_name: state.name,
            city_name: city.city_name,
          });
        }
      });
    });
  });

  return results;
};

How does this work?
Suppose the search text is Karnataka.

The loop checks the state name.

It checks each city inside that state.

It checks each location inside each city.

Since every location belongs to Karnataka, every location is added to results.

If the search is Koramangala, only the location whose name matches is added.

Step 3: Display the results
Create the search results based on whether the user has entered text:

let displayList = [];

if (searchText.trim() !== '') {
  displayList = getSearchResults();
} else if (!selectedState) {
  displayList = states;
} else if (!selectedCity) {
  displayList = cityList;
} else {
  displayList = locationList;
}

This preserves your existing browsing behavior when the search bar is empty.

One important detail: When search results are displayed, each result needs its state_name and city_name so the UI can show the correct parent city and state below the location name.

Your next step is to connect a search result's onPress to the correct state and city, rather than treating every result as if it were already at the location level.

